          "label": "sonnet-baseline"
        },
        "response": {
          "output": "Not until you know why it fails. Loosening is only fine if the assert was stricter than the real requirement, for example an exact float match or an ordering that was never guaranteed.\n\nCheck which case you're in:\n- **The code is wrong:** the assert is catching a real bug, and loosening it hides the bug. Fix the code.\n- **The assert is wrong:** it encodes a requirement that doesn't exist. Loosen it to what the code actually promises, and don't just widen it until it passes.\n- **The test is flaky:** the failure depends on timing, ordering, or randomness. Make the test deterministic rather than loosening the assert.\n\nDoes the failing value look like a real regression, or like noise? If you paste the assert and its failure output, I can tell you which case it is."
        },
