        "response": {
          "output": "Build the evals first. That's the point of eval-driven development (EDD).\n\n1. Write 5–10 cases from real or realistic inputs, including the failures you already expect. Each case needs a pass/fail check.\n2. Run a one-line baseline prompt against them. That score is your starting point.\n3. Improve the prompt and re-run after each change. Add a case every time you find a new failure.\n\nIf you write the prompt first, you'll tune it to the examples in your head and have no way to tell whether a later change helped or hurt. The evals can be rough at the start.\n\nThe `prompt-edd` skill in this toolkit covers this workflow if you want the full loop."
